WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.600
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.909
There was a previous episode on a technique
where the inputs were a source video of ourselves,

00:00:09.909 --> 00:00:16.420
and a target actor. And the output was a video
of this target actor with our facial gestures.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:21.980
With such an algorithm, one can edit pre-recorded
videos in real time, and the current version

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.830
only needs a consumer webcam to do that.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:31.619
This new version addresses two major shortcomings:
One: the previous work relied on depth information,

00:00:31.619 --> 00:00:36.120
which means that we needed to know how far
different parts of the image were from the

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:41.519
camera. This newer version only relies on
color information and does not need anything

00:00:41.519 --> 00:00:47.129
beyond that. Whoa!
Two: Previous techniques often resorted to

00:00:47.129 --> 00:00:53.089
copying the footage from the mouth and adding
synthetic proxies for teeth. Not anymore with

00:00:53.089 --> 00:00:53.780
this one!

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:58.870
I tip my hat to the authors, who came up with
a vastly improved version of their previous

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:05.040
method so quickly, and it is probably needless
to say that the ramifications of such an existing

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:09.690
technique are far reaching, and are hopefully
pointed in a positive direction.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:15.350
However, we should bear in mind that from
now on, we may be one step closer to an era

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:19.860
where a video of something happening won't
be taken as proper evidence. I wonder how

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:23.030
this will affect legal decision-making in
the future.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:27.390
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

